1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,240
Life below the sea.

2
00:00:03,241 --> 00:00:04,500
Go deep, go deep, go deep!

3
00:00:04,501 --> 00:00:05,201
They're starting!

4
00:00:05,202 --> 00:00:09,040
It's never been safe for submariners.

5
00:00:09,041 --> 00:00:12,840
Fire.

6
00:00:12,841 --> 00:00:14,040
Flood.

7
00:00:14,041 --> 00:00:15,040
Explosions.

8
00:00:15,041 --> 00:00:17,040
Lives snuffed out.

9
00:00:17,041 --> 00:00:18,040
Thresher.

10
00:00:18,041 --> 00:00:19,040
Scorpion.

11
00:00:19,041 --> 00:00:20,040
Coorset.

12
00:00:20,041 --> 00:00:24,040
Catastrophic events seared into memory.

13
00:00:24,041 --> 00:00:26,040
Hard lessons.

14
00:00:26,041 --> 00:00:29,040
Now, sub-disasters.

15
00:00:29,041 --> 00:00:32,040
Sub Disasters on Modern Marbles.

16
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:49,240
No matter how calm the waters may be on the surface,

17
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:56,700
down below, with awesome power and silent fury,

18
00:00:56,701 --> 00:01:00,200
they can destroy the submariner and his steel vessel in a heartbeat.

19
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:06,940
It's been said the sea is a more hostile environment than space.

20
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:11,940
Submarines operate in an inherently dangerous environment.

21
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:17,040
They operate deep under the ocean, under tremendous pressures,

22
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:24,140
from the compressed ocean water, and any mistake, any technical malfunction,

23
00:01:24,141 --> 00:01:26,780
can potentially be fatal.

24
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:28,980
You can't bail out as you might in an airplane.

25
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:31,420
You can't lower the boats as you might on the surface.

26
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:34,360
You can't jump off the deck of a carrier

27
00:01:34,361 --> 00:01:36,040
and hope to survive and get picked up.

28
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:38,360
There's no place to go.

29
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:53,140
Any system malfunction can potentially lead to a loss of power.

30
00:01:54,140 --> 00:02:03,580
If this occurs, the ship loses its maneuverability, its ability to generate breathable air, and ultimately, its ability to surface.

31
00:02:09,220 --> 00:02:19,300
Out of power, out of control, in a worst-case scenario, it sinks to its crush depth, where the structure can no longer withstand the increasing water pressure.

32
00:02:19,301 --> 00:02:24,700
an entire crew perishes

33
00:02:24,701 --> 00:02:31,240
but this triggers a vital response within the submarine community

34
00:02:31,241 --> 00:02:35,180
the basic cycle goes the first we find out that something's gone wrong

35
00:02:35,181 --> 00:02:38,040
then there's an investigation we try to figure out why it went wrong

36
00:02:38,041 --> 00:02:41,760
the investigation will lead to some sort of findings as to the root cause

37
00:02:41,761 --> 00:02:45,100
and that gives us an idea as to what we need to do to try to make things better

38
00:02:45,101 --> 00:02:51,617
In 1968, mindful of successes and failures, the U.S.

39
00:02:51,618 --> 00:02:58,760
Navy was moving ahead with its nuclear submarine program.

40
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:11,400
Five years earlier, it had suffered the loss of Thresher, with all 129 men aboard.

41
00:03:11,401 --> 00:03:20,080
But after an investigation, it had implemented changes aimed at advancing the cause of submarine safety.

42
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:31,420
On May 21, 1968, all was well with the Scorpion, a nuclear sub with 99 men aboard.

43
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:37,380
The fast attack sub had left the Mediterranean after a successful mission shadowing the Soviet fleet.

44
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:42,460
It radioed its position, about 50 miles south of the Azores.

45
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:49,880
But on May 22nd, it lay in pieces on the bottom of the ocean,

46
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:54,740
under 10,000 feet of water, its crew of 99 all dead.

47
00:03:55,500 --> 00:03:57,640
The loss of the scorpion was a huge shock

48
00:03:57,641 --> 00:04:00,300
because it did come after all these improvements had been made

49
00:04:00,301 --> 00:04:01,620
after the thresher was lost.

50
00:04:01,621 --> 00:04:08,940
The story of the Scorpion is one of those painful episodes

51
00:04:08,941 --> 00:04:10,700
that defies the notion of closure.

52
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:15,840
Many questions, few answers.

53
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:21,500
There's no way of finding out what the crew was going through

54
00:04:21,501 --> 00:04:23,100
in the last moments of their lives.

55
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:30,600
They don't have cockpit voice recorders in some reason.

56
00:04:30,601 --> 00:04:32,500
There's no way to know that.

57
00:04:34,440 --> 00:04:37,720
The Scorpion was not in contact with people at the time that it sank.

58
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:41,500
There have been rumors ranging from Russian submarines torpedoing it

59
00:04:41,501 --> 00:04:45,400
to a battery well explosion to a hot run in the torpedo tube

60
00:04:45,401 --> 00:04:47,640
that caused the torpedo to go off prematurely.

61
00:04:54,020 --> 00:04:57,260
Two decades later, the Navy still wanted to know

62
00:04:57,261 --> 00:04:59,220
what had happened to its men and their sub.

63
00:05:00,140 --> 00:05:04,520
It built Jason Jr., a deep-sea robot, which was sent down to investigate.

64
00:05:06,620 --> 00:05:09,280
And sure enough, there was an explosion in the forward torpedo room.

65
00:05:20,540 --> 00:05:25,420
While some believe the Soviet sunk Scorpion in retaliation for one of theirs we may have sunk,

66
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:31,220
The official Navy theory focuses on a faulty battery in the compartment next to the torpedo room.

67
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:41,340
A now-declassified Navy report from 1987 speculates that a battery explosion fits the damage to the submarine.

68
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:47,540
There is a tremendous amount of energy stored in a submarine battery,

69
00:05:47,541 --> 00:05:52,780
even a small one like what we had on nuclear power boats, that could cause a fairly serious explosion.

70
00:05:52,781 --> 00:05:57,340
That could have triggered a subsequent explosion in the torpedo room,

71
00:05:57,680 --> 00:05:59,820
which would have blown a huge hole in Scorpion.

72
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:03,660
It would have flooded rapidly and then sunk to its crushed depth.

73
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:08,080
When you puncture the hull of a submarine at that kind of a depth,

74
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:12,240
the rapid change in air pressure causes the air to ignite,

75
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:14,060
essentially snuffs out everybody aboard.

76
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,500
But at 10,000 feet on the ocean bottom,

77
00:06:22,340 --> 00:06:25,860
The details of Scorpion's demise may forever remain a mystery.

78
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:29,520
You know, you can't go down there and look at it.

79
00:06:29,660 --> 00:06:32,140
You can run a submersible down there and take pictures,

80
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:36,780
but you can't actually go down and touch it like you could an air crash, for example.

81
00:06:37,340 --> 00:06:41,320
So any submarine sinking, there's always a great deal of mystery around it.

82
00:06:41,321 --> 00:06:55,280
Nonetheless, the Navy forged ahead, acting on the most plausible theory explaining the

83
00:06:55,281 --> 00:06:56,660
Scorpion's untimely end.

84
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:03,580
About a year after this submarine disaster, Naval Ordnance ordered a redesign and replacement

85
00:07:03,581 --> 00:07:06,160
of the torpedo battery that had been red flagged.

86
00:07:06,161 --> 00:07:14,300
So whether that was the cause or not, the search for an answer led to an improved technology that made subs even safer.

87
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:16,860
Dive. Dive.

88
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:21,460
Dive.

89
00:07:21,840 --> 00:07:26,713
Since the Scorpion submarine tragedy, there have been no subs lost by the U.S.

90
00:07:26,714 --> 00:07:27,900
in nearly 40 years.

91
00:07:30,460 --> 00:07:33,680
But that wasn't the case in the beginning of submarine history.

92
00:07:33,681 --> 00:07:40,680
The first three submarines enlisted into military service all encountered trouble.

93
00:07:40,681 --> 00:07:47,680
During the American Revolution, George Washington's forces included a one-man submarine called

94
00:07:47,681 --> 00:07:48,413
the Turtle.

95
00:07:48,414 --> 00:07:53,680
Its mission was to attach an explosive to a British warship in New York Harbor.

96
00:07:53,681 --> 00:07:57,680
It failed, although the submariner survived.

97
00:07:57,681 --> 00:08:06,420
In the American Civil War, submarines were once again called into service.

98
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:10,380
The Union Navy developed a sub called the Alligator,

99
00:08:10,980 --> 00:08:15,200
but it never saw active duty as it was lost in transport during a storm.

100
00:08:17,580 --> 00:08:23,420
But by far the Civil War's most compelling submarine story centers on a Confederate vessel.

101
00:08:23,421 --> 00:08:31,440
On a chilly February night in 1864, eight men squeezed on board the CSS H.L. Hunley

102
00:08:31,441 --> 00:08:35,220
for a mission below the waters of Charleston Bay, South Carolina.

103
00:08:37,300 --> 00:08:47,640
Essentially, it was a pipe about 40 feet long, and the crew, the eight unfortunates who made

104
00:08:47,641 --> 00:08:54,520
up the motive power of the thing, sat on a bench, and operated a crank which turned the propeller.

105
00:08:56,520 --> 00:09:00,360
Despite its spartan accommodations, the Hunley was advanced for its time.

106
00:09:01,320 --> 00:09:06,200
It had a rudimentary bellow system for its snorkel, diving planes, and ballast tanks.

107
00:09:06,201 --> 00:09:18,140
The mission of the Hunley and its crew was to sink the USS Housatonic by attaching its

108
00:09:18,141 --> 00:09:20,020
spar torpedo to the ship's hull.

109
00:09:22,320 --> 00:09:28,400
The torpedo was attached to the end of the spar, and it was a copper canister filled

110
00:09:28,401 --> 00:09:30,480
with 75 pounds of black powder.

111
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:34,840
And it was attached to a barb, much like a thimble.

112
00:09:34,841 --> 00:09:43,220
So when they ran the barb into the ship and they pulled back, the canister would remain and then the bar would pull out.

113
00:09:43,660 --> 00:09:48,560
And so they would go back 100 yards and then there was a reel on the Hunley.

114
00:09:48,640 --> 00:09:52,420
And when they got to the end of the 100 yards, that would ignite the powder.

115
00:09:54,280 --> 00:09:56,460
That's exactly what the Hunley's crew did.

116
00:09:56,960 --> 00:10:01,760
And when the explosion occurred, just as planned, the Housatonic began to sink.

117
00:10:01,761 --> 00:10:06,700
The Hunley had pulled off the world's first successful submarine combat mission.

118
00:10:07,660 --> 00:10:12,040
This much we know, but the rest of the story was incomplete for over a century.

119
00:10:12,680 --> 00:10:15,500
Well, the mystery was that the Hunley didn't return to port.

120
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:17,860
And so what happened to the Hunley?

121
00:10:19,580 --> 00:10:24,000
The next day, the Confederates could see the mass of the Housatonic sticking out of the water,

122
00:10:24,080 --> 00:10:25,600
but the Hunley never came back.

123
00:10:25,601 --> 00:10:35,360
Remarkably, after a 15-year quest, the Hunley was discovered in 1995 by a team of underwater

124
00:10:35,361 --> 00:10:38,840
archaeologists under the direction of author Clive Kussler.

125
00:10:55,600 --> 00:11:02,920
A special harness was designed and built for the delicate process of raising and investigating

126
00:11:02,921 --> 00:11:07,160
this amazing piece of history.

127
00:11:07,161 --> 00:11:18,160
And it was about a thousand feet off the Housatani.

128
00:11:18,161 --> 00:11:23,000
And what it turned out to be that it was waiting for the tide to turn because it didn't sink

129
00:11:23,001 --> 00:11:24,947
obviously from the explosion.

130
00:11:24,948 --> 00:11:28,840
And I believe that the Canandaiga, which was a ship coming

131
00:11:28,841 --> 00:11:35,000
to rescue the Housatonic survivors who were up in the rigging, struck the Hunley and sank it.

132
00:11:36,120 --> 00:11:41,320
The fate of the Hunley showed that the technology was still too rudimentary and unsafe for widespread

133
00:11:41,321 --> 00:11:42,312
practical use.

134
00:11:42,313 --> 00:11:48,120
With only man-powered propulsion, the crew of the Hunley couldn't get away in time

135
00:11:48,121 --> 00:11:49,258
to survive.

136
00:11:49,259 --> 00:11:57,220
Four decades later, an inventor and entrepreneur named John Holland developed

137
00:11:57,221 --> 00:12:01,640
a practical vessel for the U.S. Navy that was ultimately known as the forerunner of

138
00:12:01,641 --> 00:12:05,612
the modern submarine. At first, steam engines were tried.

139
00:12:05,613 --> 00:12:08,120
While powerful, they had a drawback.

140
00:12:08,121 --> 00:12:14,388
The negative was that, of course, the thing became like a blast furnace.

141
00:12:14,389 --> 00:12:15,520
It was so hot

142
00:12:15,521 --> 00:12:18,540
and the crew suffered accordingly.

143
00:12:20,120 --> 00:12:23,940
After five prototypes, Holland built the USS Holland,

144
00:12:24,640 --> 00:12:26,540
a six-man sub for the U.S. Navy.

145
00:12:29,040 --> 00:12:31,320
It featured a dual-engine system,

146
00:12:32,020 --> 00:12:33,700
gasoline power for surface cruising,

147
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:36,380
and an electric motor for submerged travel.

148
00:12:37,680 --> 00:12:39,580
But these had inherent dangers.

149
00:12:40,300 --> 00:12:43,080
For one thing, the battery for the electric motor could explode.

150
00:12:43,081 --> 00:12:51,820
And if seawater got into the battery, it would produce deadly chlorine gas, the same kind of gas used in World War I in the trenches to kill enemy soldiers.

151
00:12:53,140 --> 00:12:55,820
But it wasn't just the batteries that posed a danger.

152
00:12:57,260 --> 00:13:00,700
Fumes from the internal combustion engines could also cause explosions.

153
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:08,320
Gasoline explosions accounted for a number of submarines sinking in the early years of the 20th century.

154
00:13:08,321 --> 00:13:13,780
To deal with this, submarine engineers switched to diesel.

155
00:13:14,240 --> 00:13:16,720
Diesel is not highly volatile.

156
00:13:17,720 --> 00:13:24,940
Diesel is relatively hard to ignite, so it's a fairly safe fuel, and much, much safer than gasoline.

157
00:13:26,400 --> 00:13:30,840
Still, there were many submarine disasters in the early years of the 20th century.

158
00:13:31,980 --> 00:13:35,480
Fire, flood, and collisions all resulted in loss of lives.

159
00:13:36,420 --> 00:13:39,980
When mishaps occurred, there was no rescue technology in place.

160
00:13:40,900 --> 00:13:43,940
That would come only after two prominent disasters,

161
00:13:44,500 --> 00:13:48,360
inspired a visionary commander named Charles Momsen.

162
00:13:51,840 --> 00:13:57,180
When the USS Scorpion went down, it was armed with 23 torpedoes.

163
00:13:57,181 --> 00:13:59,840
Two of them nuclear-tipped.

164
00:13:59,841 --> 00:14:13,841
On September 25, 1925, an American submarine, the USS S-51, collided with a cargo ship off the coast of Rhode Island and went down in 130 feet of water.

165
00:14:19,580 --> 00:14:27,820
Commander Charles Swede Momsen was ordered to take his sub, the USS S-1, out to search for the crippled vessel.

166
00:14:29,840 --> 00:14:34,840
When he found it, he soon realized there were survivors.

167
00:14:34,940 --> 00:14:36,640
He knew there were people alive in there.

168
00:14:36,740 --> 00:14:37,840
They were banging on the hull.

169
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:38,840
You could hear them.

170
00:14:38,940 --> 00:14:41,080
And there wasn't a thing he could do for them.

171
00:14:41,180 --> 00:14:43,180
And there wasn't a thing they could do for themselves.

172
00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:44,980
They had no way of getting out the boat.

173
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:59,100
Two years later, when a second sub, the USS S-4, was lost,

174
00:14:59,101 --> 00:15:01,240
Momsen was spurred to take action.

175
00:15:01,720 --> 00:15:04,740
He sees the inadequacy of the measures taken,

176
00:15:05,120 --> 00:15:07,840
and so he decides that they need more sophisticated equipment,

177
00:15:08,160 --> 00:15:10,500
and he goes on to invent two types of devices

178
00:15:10,501 --> 00:15:13,960
that will be very important to the saving of submarine crews in the future.

179
00:15:13,961 --> 00:15:14,900
Test out the lung.

180
00:15:15,380 --> 00:15:20,700
In 1929, he first tested what was to become the Momsen lung.

181
00:15:21,720 --> 00:15:24,260
Momsen's device was an oblong rubber bag

182
00:15:24,261 --> 00:15:27,260
with two tubes that recycled exhaled air.

183
00:15:28,140 --> 00:15:30,640
The lung contained a canister of soda lime,

184
00:15:30,641 --> 00:15:33,520
which removed carbon dioxide from exhaled air

185
00:15:33,521 --> 00:15:35,440
and then replenished it with oxygen.

186
00:15:37,820 --> 00:15:41,800
Soon, sailors were using the Momsen lung in disaster simulation training,

187
00:15:42,120 --> 00:15:44,400
in which they learned to escape from a submarine.

188
00:15:45,140 --> 00:15:47,920
The original simulation device was the tower

189
00:15:47,921 --> 00:15:51,640
that let submariners practice entering the water

190
00:15:51,641 --> 00:15:55,460
from a depth of from 50 to 100 to 150 feet

191
00:15:55,461 --> 00:16:01,160
and to feel what it was like and to experience what it's like to use this breathing device,

192
00:16:01,280 --> 00:16:05,060
the Momsen lung, as they ascended from the depths to the surface.

193
00:16:08,360 --> 00:16:13,440
In 1929, the Navy then pushed for the development of a rescue chamber.

194
00:16:14,340 --> 00:16:19,340
Begun by Momsen and later modified by a colleague, Lieutenant Commander Alan McCann,

195
00:16:19,880 --> 00:16:23,060
the Navy built a large, steel, bell-shaped chamber,

196
00:16:23,061 --> 00:16:27,120
which could be lowered with cables down to the escape hatch on a sunken submarine.

197
00:16:28,120 --> 00:16:30,820
A watertight seal to the submarine could be created

198
00:16:30,821 --> 00:16:35,640
by placing a rubber gasket around the diving bell's bottom and reducing the air pressure.

199
00:16:37,500 --> 00:16:41,520
The hatch could then be opened, and the trapped submariners could climb aboard.

200
00:16:42,420 --> 00:16:45,320
A hatch on the top of the bell would allow the rescued submariners

201
00:16:45,321 --> 00:16:47,960
and diving bell operators an easy way out.

202
00:16:47,961 --> 00:16:51,740
They were trying to perfect ways of getting out of the submarine,

203
00:16:51,741 --> 00:16:55,000
but it hadn't been tried in an emergency situation.

204
00:16:56,240 --> 00:17:00,620
It was only a matter of time before Momsen's equipment would be put to the test.

205
00:17:01,580 --> 00:17:06,980
In 1939, the United States Navy launched its newest submarine, the USS Squalus.

206
00:17:08,660 --> 00:17:10,331
But it had new technology.

207
00:17:10,332 --> 00:17:14,060
It was the latest thing, and not everything was perfected.

208
00:17:14,940 --> 00:17:19,300
In its very first year, the USS Squalus had been refitted

209
00:17:19,301 --> 00:17:23,640
and was carrying out her post-refit trials off the coast of Portsmouth, New Hampshire.

210
00:17:26,940 --> 00:17:30,460
Its problems began with a malfunction of the main induction valve,

211
00:17:30,820 --> 00:17:36,200
a 36-inch opening in the hull that provided air needed for the operation of the diesel engines.

212
00:17:37,160 --> 00:17:42,000
Just before diving, the indicator showed that it was closed and safe to submerge.

213
00:17:42,440 --> 00:17:46,800
But the valve was open, and that flooded out the engine room.

214
00:17:49,300 --> 00:17:54,820
And it happened so quickly that there was no way they could counter the effect of the inrush of water.

215
00:17:56,480 --> 00:18:00,240
The sub was down 243 feet below the surface.

216
00:18:01,580 --> 00:18:04,060
28 men died almost immediately.

217
00:18:04,540 --> 00:18:07,080
33 members of the crew, however, do survive.

218
00:18:08,540 --> 00:18:15,420
They're in a watertight compartment, and they release a small float with a radio telephone that floats to the surface.

219
00:18:15,421 --> 00:18:23,420
The captain of the Suelus tells the people on the surface that his crew survives and

220
00:18:23,421 --> 00:18:24,420
that they need rescue.

221
00:18:24,421 --> 00:18:26,420
Then the phone line is cut.

222
00:18:26,421 --> 00:18:31,420
So these men in these three compartments were still alive.

223
00:18:31,421 --> 00:18:36,420
They were far below anything that any man had ever been rescued from before.

224
00:18:36,421 --> 00:18:40,420
They spread soda lime out on the decks to absorb CO2 in the air.

225
00:18:40,421 --> 00:18:47,420
The men not actually required to do something were told to go lie down, conserve energy,

226
00:18:47,421 --> 00:18:53,420
and conserve oxygen, and they waited for rescue to come.

227
00:18:53,421 --> 00:19:02,420
When the Navy finally realized what had happened, six agonizing hours later, a rescue party was organized

228
00:19:02,421 --> 00:19:08,420
under the guidance of none other than Charles Swede Monson, who was flown in from Washington, D.C.

229
00:19:08,421 --> 00:19:15,520
His invention, the McCann Rescue Chamber, was to be utilized in an attempt to save all the surviving submariners.

230
00:19:17,220 --> 00:19:22,540
There were three compartments with air in them, but the middle compartment was the forward battery compartment.

231
00:19:23,060 --> 00:19:28,560
They were afraid that seawater would get into the battery and produce poison gas, so the men sealed that off.

232
00:19:28,561 --> 00:19:36,580
So there was the control room with half the men in it, then this empty space, and then the forward torpedo room with the other half of the men.

233
00:19:38,420 --> 00:19:43,420
Eventually, the worst-case scenario occurred in the forward battery compartment.

234
00:19:43,421 --> 00:19:49,740
As water seeped into the squalas, it mixed with the acid in the batteries and produced

235
00:19:49,741 --> 00:19:52,620
chlorine gas.

236
00:19:52,621 --> 00:20:00,980
But they used the Mopsin lung as a gas mask, and it was very effective.

237
00:20:00,981 --> 00:20:05,180
Meanwhile on the surface, the Navy had gotten its ships, men, and equipment ready for the

238
00:20:05,181 --> 00:20:06,180
rescue.

239
00:20:06,181 --> 00:20:14,880
They were then able to hover the diving bell down and seal it against the hull and begin to carry the men up.

240
00:20:15,740 --> 00:20:19,220
Initially, they thought it would take five trips to bring all the men to the service.

241
00:20:20,520 --> 00:20:24,280
But something told Momsen, don't play it safe.

242
00:20:25,300 --> 00:20:29,800
So he had them overload the bell and get them up in four trips instead of five.

243
00:20:29,801 --> 00:20:36,680
On the fourth trip, the cable bringing them up broke.

244
00:20:37,680 --> 00:20:39,200
The bell was cast loose.

245
00:20:42,020 --> 00:20:44,340
It had one thin strand left.

246
00:20:46,980 --> 00:20:51,680
They got 50 or so crewmen to hold the cable and pull it up by hand.

247
00:20:51,681 --> 00:21:01,640
If he'd gone with five missions instead of four, the last group of men would have been

248
00:21:01,641 --> 00:21:03,360
left trapped on the bottom with no way out.

249
00:21:07,640 --> 00:21:12,210
It's a spectacular success for the U.S. Navy, and it's a true testament to the U.S.

250
00:21:12,211 --> 00:21:12,911
Navy's

251
00:21:12,912 --> 00:21:16,100
valuable investment in rescue technology.

252
00:21:16,101 --> 00:21:24,560
All the equipment was right there on hand to be used, and it had been developed and was ready to go.

253
00:21:25,180 --> 00:21:28,940
And the experienced personnel were available to make it work.

254
00:21:31,820 --> 00:21:34,860
The rescue was also a victory for Charles Momsen.

255
00:21:35,960 --> 00:21:39,348
Momsen was an innovator. He was a true believer.

256
00:21:39,349 --> 00:21:43,160
He was the kind of person who makes a real difference.

257
00:21:43,161 --> 00:21:47,160
When he came along, nobody had ever been rescued from a sunken sub.

258
00:21:47,161 --> 00:21:49,160
Nobody thought it was possible.

259
00:21:49,161 --> 00:21:52,160
And he said, I'm going to change that. And he did.

260
00:21:52,161 --> 00:22:00,160
Eventually, the squalus was raised, refloated, repaired, and recommissioned as the USS Sailfish.

261
00:22:00,161 --> 00:22:08,160
Improvements in submarine systems ensured that the malfunctions that initially put the squalus at risk would not reoccur.

262
00:22:08,161 --> 00:22:13,160
But the world's superpowers would soon be involved in a Cold War

263
00:22:13,161 --> 00:22:18,160
that would push submarine technology beyond any previous limits of safety.

264
00:22:21,160 --> 00:22:26,160
The Squalus could travel 20 knots on the surface, 9 knots submerged.

265
00:22:26,161 --> 00:22:30,160
She'd made 18 successful test dives on the day she sank.

266
00:22:30,161 --> 00:22:39,700
During the Cold War, the United States and the Soviet Union were engaged in an escalating

267
00:22:39,701 --> 00:22:41,060
and frightening arms race.

268
00:22:43,700 --> 00:22:50,280
It was the nuclear age, and during the 1960s, both sides seemed on the brink of annihilating

269
00:22:50,281 --> 00:22:52,300
one another, if not the world.

270
00:22:54,740 --> 00:22:58,560
This was the same era as the Cuban Missile Crisis, the Berlin Crisis.

271
00:22:58,561 --> 00:23:01,720
people thought there was going to be a nuclear war with Russia,

272
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:05,240
and every new piece of military technology was vital.

273
00:23:05,900 --> 00:23:09,100
What this meant was that things were rushed into service real quick.

274
00:23:11,700 --> 00:23:15,960
And that included submarines, which were also entering the nuclear age.

275
00:23:16,820 --> 00:23:21,700
A nuclear-powered sub was more efficient and could remain below the water's surface longer,

276
00:23:22,320 --> 00:23:25,460
and therefore remain undetected for longer periods of time.

277
00:23:25,461 --> 00:23:32,080
With a nuclear plant, your submarine could go as long as the groceries last.

278
00:23:33,040 --> 00:23:34,380
That's your only limitation.

279
00:23:35,340 --> 00:23:36,800
That could be up to six months.

280
00:23:37,960 --> 00:23:40,660
And it stays timed. And it's fast.

281
00:23:41,600 --> 00:23:46,540
It ended up to a war machine that could secretly get closer to enemy territory than ever before,

282
00:23:47,080 --> 00:23:50,440
and then launch its missiles, if it came to that.

283
00:23:50,441 --> 00:23:58,080
So when submarines switched to nuclear power plants, as with the Nautilus, and soon afterwards

284
00:23:58,081 --> 00:24:10,440
started carrying nuclear weapons, the magnitude of potential disasters increased exponentially.

285
00:24:10,441 --> 00:24:12,660
It's been a deadly game.

286
00:24:12,661 --> 00:24:17,980
The Russians have lost five nuclear subs, and the U.S. has lost two.

287
00:24:17,981 --> 00:24:30,080
In every case, all men aboard have perished.

288
00:24:30,081 --> 00:24:36,640
The story of the U.S. Navy's Thresher is a painful chapter from the Cold War era.

289
00:24:36,641 --> 00:24:44,480
The fast attack sub was the 15th nuclear sub launched and the lead ship of the Permit class.

290
00:24:44,481 --> 00:24:47,480
in the morning of April 10th, 1963,

291
00:24:47,481 --> 00:24:51,020
the sub was on a test dive off the coast of Cape Cod,

292
00:24:51,021 --> 00:24:55,600
accompanied by the US rescue ship Skylark.

293
00:24:55,601 --> 00:24:57,960
It was going to what's called test depth,

294
00:24:57,961 --> 00:25:02,800
which is the maximum safe depth that a submarine can sail at.

295
00:25:02,801 --> 00:25:05,500
And it was in constant contact with the surface

296
00:25:05,501 --> 00:25:06,760
as it went through this dive.

297
00:25:09,680 --> 00:25:12,080
But at one point, Thresher signaled a message

298
00:25:12,081 --> 00:25:15,020
that it was having minor problems.

299
00:25:15,021 --> 00:25:17,700
This would be the last contact with the sub.

300
00:25:17,701 --> 00:25:19,880
My guess would be that the people on board

301
00:25:19,881 --> 00:25:21,100
were working through these things,

302
00:25:21,101 --> 00:25:23,240
that there was probably not a lot of panic,

303
00:25:23,241 --> 00:25:24,860
that they were just trying harder and harder

304
00:25:24,861 --> 00:25:27,920
to save things until they realized that they couldn't.

305
00:25:30,660 --> 00:25:32,660
The Navy would later conclude that the trouble

306
00:25:32,661 --> 00:25:35,480
began the previous summer, when Thresher

307
00:25:35,481 --> 00:25:38,560
had suffered some damage to 28 joints on its piping

308
00:25:38,561 --> 00:25:39,600
during shock testing.

309
00:25:42,080 --> 00:25:46,240
Others speculated that an earlier collision with a tugboat was another possible culprit.

310
00:25:47,560 --> 00:25:54,020
In either case, during Thresher's dive to test depth, a joint on a small pipe began to let water in.

311
00:25:54,021 --> 00:25:59,800
Which immediately shorted out some electrical controls, which controlled the nuclear reactor.

312
00:26:00,780 --> 00:26:03,880
And when that shorted out, the reactor shut down.

313
00:26:06,180 --> 00:26:11,060
Out of power, the sub could no longer communicate with rescue ship Skylark.

314
00:26:11,760 --> 00:26:13,700
At this point, it began to sink.

315
00:26:14,080 --> 00:26:15,320
Submarines work like airplanes.

316
00:26:15,420 --> 00:26:18,000
As they move through the water, they have a certain buoyancy.

317
00:26:18,120 --> 00:26:19,020
It helps them keep up.

318
00:26:19,520 --> 00:26:21,420
When they lost power, it started sinking.

319
00:26:21,900 --> 00:26:25,100
And it was already at test depth, the maximum safe depth.

320
00:26:30,280 --> 00:26:33,240
The emergency blow system could have helped them to surface.

321
00:26:34,080 --> 00:26:35,400
But it, too, failed.

322
00:26:36,220 --> 00:26:40,280
This is a process in which compressed air is directed to different sections of the sub

323
00:26:40,281 --> 00:26:42,680
in order to control surfacing and diving.

324
00:26:43,020 --> 00:26:45,720
The way that the emergency blow system was set up then

325
00:26:45,721 --> 00:26:48,640
was going through relatively narrow pipes with a lot of turns

326
00:26:48,641 --> 00:26:50,660
that went through pressure-reducing valves,

327
00:26:50,880 --> 00:26:53,740
and the system froze up, so they couldn't get high-pressure air

328
00:26:53,741 --> 00:26:55,600
into the ballast tanks to blow the water out,

329
00:26:55,900 --> 00:26:57,620
and they had no way to get to the surface.

330
00:27:01,720 --> 00:27:03,660
The men must have been aware of trouble,

331
00:27:04,440 --> 00:27:06,260
but just kept trying to save the vessel.

332
00:27:06,860 --> 00:27:08,160
They knew something was happening.

333
00:27:08,280 --> 00:27:10,120
They probably didn't know what it was exactly.

334
00:27:10,280 --> 00:27:14,600
They were probably doing their jobs as they were trained to do them.

335
00:27:14,601 --> 00:27:21,400
But before the men could restore the electrical circuits, thresher sank to crush depth.

336
00:27:21,401 --> 00:27:26,900
When they get to crush depth, what happens is the sea pressure so quickly crushes the

337
00:27:26,901 --> 00:27:27,900
submarine.

338
00:27:27,901 --> 00:27:29,120
It gives way all at once.

339
00:27:29,121 --> 00:27:34,860
The air is compressed so quickly and so intensely that it bursts into flames.

340
00:27:34,861 --> 00:27:37,380
So the men on those submarines didn't die of drowning.

341
00:27:37,381 --> 00:27:38,380
They were burned to death.

342
00:27:38,381 --> 00:27:40,620
In fact, they were incinerated in a burst of light.

343
00:27:46,140 --> 00:27:48,360
129 men lost their lives that morning.

344
00:27:49,180 --> 00:27:53,240
They went down with their sub to the ocean bottom at some 8,000 feet.

345
00:27:53,540 --> 00:27:56,000
In the submarine community, it was a shock.

346
00:27:56,520 --> 00:28:03,180
It was a shock quite different from the shock it presented to the families and relatives of the lost crewmen.

347
00:28:03,760 --> 00:28:05,320
It shouldn't have happened.

348
00:28:05,321 --> 00:28:09,420
it's the sort of thing that's not supposed to happen.

349
00:28:09,880 --> 00:28:11,360
We found out that we weren't perfect,

350
00:28:11,660 --> 00:28:14,480
that we hadn't learned as much about submarine design as we thought,

351
00:28:14,481 --> 00:28:16,740
and that we could still lose even the newest boats.

352
00:28:20,460 --> 00:28:23,340
Thresher's nuclear reactor was not damaged in the disaster

353
00:28:23,341 --> 00:28:25,960
and remains intact at the bottom of the sea.

354
00:28:26,920 --> 00:28:29,020
Furthermore, there are no lost nuclear weapons,

355
00:28:29,220 --> 00:28:31,180
as she was on a test mission and not armed.

356
00:28:31,181 --> 00:28:39,040
The Thresher tragedy led to a number of changes aimed towards averting disaster.

357
00:28:39,041 --> 00:28:43,800
The implementation of better safety standards in nuclear subs, a means of restarting the

358
00:28:43,801 --> 00:28:48,800
nuclear reactor in case of emergency, and a redesign of the ballast blow tank system.

359
00:28:48,801 --> 00:28:53,940
So you had large diameter pipes that did not reduce the pressure, that just blew high pressure

360
00:28:53,941 --> 00:29:00,900
air into the ballast tanks to blow the water out to get you up to the surface in a hurry.

361
00:29:00,901 --> 00:29:05,940
When Thresher plunged its sailors to the sub's crush depth, there was as yet no technology

362
00:29:05,941 --> 00:29:10,680
to rescue a downed sub that was far below sea level, but that hadn't reached its breaking

363
00:29:10,681 --> 00:29:11,680
point.

364
00:29:11,681 --> 00:29:18,400
The deep submergence rescue vehicles, or DSRVs, were created as a direct result of the

365
00:29:18,401 --> 00:29:22,340
loss of Thresher in April of 1963.

366
00:29:22,341 --> 00:29:26,880
The Navy realized that even if Thresher had gone down in an area of the ocean which would

367
00:29:26,881 --> 00:29:32,040
permit access to her, we did not have the technology to go down that far, even that

368
00:29:32,041 --> 00:29:36,880
far, and bring the crew back to the surface to safety.

369
00:29:36,881 --> 00:29:40,840
The U.S. Navy developed electrically powered DSRVs.

370
00:29:40,841 --> 00:29:43,480
These can rescue up to 24 men at a

371
00:29:43,481 --> 00:29:44,181
time.

372
00:29:44,182 --> 00:29:54,160
Loaded up on a C-5 transport plane, they can get to a sub in distress in any ocean

373
00:29:54,161 --> 00:29:55,160
in a matter of hours.

374
00:29:55,161 --> 00:29:58,860
In fact, they can even be piggybacked on another submarine.

375
00:29:58,861 --> 00:30:07,840
They have cradles made for them that allow them to be piggybacked on another submarine.

376
00:30:07,841 --> 00:30:13,480
Once the DSRV locates the disabled sub, it uses its special docking system.

377
00:30:13,481 --> 00:30:18,580
The black and white ring seen here mates with the upper escape trunk hatch of the sub in

378
00:30:18,581 --> 00:30:20,580
distress.

379
00:30:20,581 --> 00:30:26,080
Then the men are taken out of the sub in groups and brought by the DSRV to another sub, or

380
00:30:26,081 --> 00:30:29,520
a surface vessel.

381
00:30:29,521 --> 00:30:36,200
Unfortunately in the year 2000, even DSRVs would be too little too late for the men aboard

382
00:30:36,201 --> 00:30:45,340
the Russian submarine, Kursk.

383
00:30:45,341 --> 00:30:50,940
The thresher broke up into six major sections, with debris scattered over 400 yards square.

384
00:30:52,780 --> 00:31:00,280
For 40 years of involvement with nuclear submarines, the Russians suffered the loss of five vessels and hundreds of lives.

385
00:31:02,880 --> 00:31:03,754
While the U.S.

386
00:31:03,755 --> 00:31:09,120
certainly made its share of mistakes, it hasn't lost a nuclear sub in nearly 40 years.

387
00:31:10,040 --> 00:31:11,200
So why the disparity?

388
00:31:11,201 --> 00:31:17,200
Communism did not reward people for doing good work, as a result of which there wasn't any point in doing good work,

389
00:31:17,201 --> 00:31:20,200
and as a result of that, they got a lot of bad work.

390
00:31:20,201 --> 00:31:24,200
They're theoretical. People were world class, and still are.

391
00:31:24,201 --> 00:31:29,200
But unless you can tighten a nut on a bolt, you don't have things break.

392
00:31:29,201 --> 00:31:31,518
During the Cold War, both the U.S.

393
00:31:31,519 --> 00:31:35,200
and Soviets were hurrying new technology into service.

394
00:31:35,201 --> 00:31:39,200
The U.S. paid the price with the losses of Thresher and Scorpion.

395
00:31:39,201 --> 00:31:41,980
And the Soviets suffered in this deadly game as well.

396
00:31:42,100 --> 00:31:45,320
They were rushing to catch up, and they cut corners.

397
00:31:45,940 --> 00:31:51,020
In addition, because of the Soviet environment, decisions were made politically.

398
00:31:51,340 --> 00:31:54,280
Mistakes could mean the end of a career, even the end of a life.

399
00:31:54,281 --> 00:31:56,200
So people did not report mistakes.

400
00:31:57,760 --> 00:32:04,440
And it bit them hard in engineering that they couldn't produce quality goods to deal with a specific task.

401
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:07,140
And that cost a lot of lives for the Soviets.

402
00:32:09,200 --> 00:32:16,200
Today, the Kursk has the painful distinction of being Russia's most famous lost nuclear sub.

403
00:32:21,200 --> 00:32:24,200
The tragic events began August 12, 2000.

404
00:32:24,201 --> 00:32:28,200
Kursk was on exercise in the Barents Sea, and she disappeared.

405
00:32:28,201 --> 00:32:37,200
She had been scheduled to fire practice torpedoes at the Russian service ships as an exercise.

406
00:32:39,200 --> 00:32:46,580
Our hydrophones and any submarines we had in the area picked up a big explosion, and

407
00:32:46,581 --> 00:32:51,340
then the Russians started looking for a lost submarine, and that was really all that we

408
00:32:51,341 --> 00:32:52,340
knew.

409
00:32:52,341 --> 00:32:59,500
A second explosion registered a 3.5 on the Richter scale, the equivalent of the

410
00:32:59,501 --> 00:33:05,820
detonation of 1.5 tons of explosives.

411
00:33:05,821 --> 00:33:09,180
The Russians knew this unfolding disaster hadn't gone unnoticed,

412
00:33:09,660 --> 00:33:14,060
but didn't alert world media until nearly 48 hours after the event.

413
00:33:14,400 --> 00:33:18,780
They were not very forthcoming right off the bat, which is understandable.

414
00:33:18,920 --> 00:33:20,600
It's embarrassing to lose a nuclear submarine.

415
00:33:21,720 --> 00:33:27,340
A Russian admiral went on television, declared that Kursk had suffered technical failures,

416
00:33:27,341 --> 00:33:33,060
had sunk, and that in all likelihood there were no survivors.

417
00:33:33,061 --> 00:33:45,560
While there were 118 men aboard, there were in fact 23 survivors, desperate to be rescued.

418
00:33:45,561 --> 00:33:54,660
The Kursk was in shallow waters, only 354 feet below the surface.

419
00:33:54,661 --> 00:34:00,820
Immediately, other nations, including the US and Great Britain, offered help.

420
00:34:00,821 --> 00:34:04,120
that the Russians rejected any assistance.

421
00:34:04,121 --> 00:34:07,000
They would do it on their own.

422
00:34:07,001 --> 00:34:09,960
I don't think that there was ever any chance of those men

423
00:34:09,961 --> 00:34:11,000
being rescued.

424
00:34:11,001 --> 00:34:13,660
The Russians were far too slow getting their resources

425
00:34:13,661 --> 00:34:14,660
on the scene.

426
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:25,080
Had everybody had the right equipment online and ready

427
00:34:25,081 --> 00:34:28,940
to go at that very moment, maybe somebody might have survived.

428
00:34:28,941 --> 00:34:30,840
That requires about four consecutive miracles.

429
00:34:31,660 --> 00:34:34,240
And emergencies happen in unexpected times.

430
00:34:34,280 --> 00:34:35,260
That's why they're called emergencies.

431
00:34:36,080 --> 00:34:39,960
In October 2001, the Russians, aided by the Dutch,

432
00:34:40,440 --> 00:34:43,480
razed the Korsk and removed her nuclear reactor and weapons.

433
00:34:44,760 --> 00:34:46,480
What caused the sinking of the Korsk?

434
00:34:47,080 --> 00:34:50,520
A Russian commission concluded the most likely cause for the explosion

435
00:34:50,521 --> 00:34:52,440
was a collision with another sub.

436
00:34:53,080 --> 00:34:54,660
They based this on two facts.

437
00:34:55,140 --> 00:34:58,480
First, the long and deep scratch marks along the side of Korsk.

438
00:34:58,940 --> 00:35:09,263
Secondly, the USS Memphis was tracked in the area at the time of the disaster, and shortly afterwards, docked in nearby Norwegian waters for emergency repairs, the

439
00:35:09,264 --> 00:35:11,860
nature of which has never been disclosed.

440
00:35:11,861 --> 00:35:25,861
while this theory is tantalizingly plausible the u.s categorically rejects it and insists the

441
00:35:28,100 --> 00:35:34,420
russians were using a dangerously volatile fuel what appears to have sunk the kursk

442
00:35:34,421 --> 00:35:40,540
was a defective torpedo fueled with htp fuel high test peroxide

443
00:35:40,541 --> 00:35:49,560
it would appear that this torpedo was damaged to the degree that some of the fuel leaked out

444
00:35:49,561 --> 00:35:56,580
of the torpedo onto the deck when htp gets on steel and oil it explodes

445
00:35:56,581 --> 00:36:02,360
what really caused this disaster will probably never be ascertained

446
00:36:02,361 --> 00:36:07,600
but the bottom line is this had those sailors had equipment to help them breathe

447
00:36:07,601 --> 00:36:10,920
They could have possibly survived until rescue crews got to them.

448
00:36:11,180 --> 00:36:15,160
They had atmosphere cleansing equipment back there of some kind,

449
00:36:15,960 --> 00:36:22,400
the Russian equivalent of an oxygen generator and a CO2 absorption unit to clean the atmosphere.

450
00:36:23,080 --> 00:36:27,740
It seems that in an attempt to get this equipment going, they started a small fire,

451
00:36:28,120 --> 00:36:33,240
which depleted the oxygen in the compartment and rendered the machinery useless.

452
00:36:33,241 --> 00:36:40,240
So they were, regrettably, they were pretty well doomed.

453
00:36:49,760 --> 00:36:56,280
To this day, six nuclear submarines, four Russian and two American, remain on the ocean

454
00:36:56,281 --> 00:36:57,780
bottom.

455
00:36:57,781 --> 00:37:04,060
They have nuclear power plants and an estimated 44 nuclear warheads, some on torpedoes, some

456
00:37:04,061 --> 00:37:10,160
on missiles.

457
00:37:10,161 --> 00:37:15,440
Navy officials and nuclear scientists claim there is little to be concerned about.

458
00:37:15,441 --> 00:37:20,360
The chance of an explosion is nearly impossible, and environmental contamination is also of

459
00:37:20,361 --> 00:37:22,740
little concern.

460
00:37:22,741 --> 00:37:27,740
Thus far, radioactive levels near the wrecks are negligible.

461
00:37:27,741 --> 00:37:29,740
Zero rating on the Geiger counter.

462
00:37:29,741 --> 00:37:32,740
Come on.

463
00:37:36,740 --> 00:37:40,740
The lost subs at the bottom of the sea will no doubt persist

464
00:37:40,741 --> 00:37:43,740
as grim reminders of what can go wrong in a submarine.

465
00:37:43,741 --> 00:37:48,740
But instead of looking back, navies around the world look forward

466
00:37:48,741 --> 00:37:55,580
what they can do to prevent disaster.

467
00:37:55,581 --> 00:38:04,540
In the area of emergency operating procedures, simulators provide the best training.

468
00:38:04,541 --> 00:38:10,860
A special Dutch barge used 26 steel cables to pull the wreckage of the 18,000 ton Korsk

469
00:38:10,861 --> 00:38:17,860
from the bottom of the Barents Sea.

470
00:38:18,980 --> 00:38:22,980
While submarines are far safer today than ever before,

471
00:38:22,981 --> 00:38:27,200
they nonetheless operate on the edge of survivability.

472
00:38:27,201 --> 00:38:34,200
Danger is never eliminated, only managed.

473
00:38:35,500 --> 00:38:39,440
Engineers continually seek to improve the technology,

474
00:38:39,441 --> 00:38:42,640
and Navy stressed the importance of training submariners

475
00:38:42,641 --> 00:38:44,700
in emergency operating procedures.

476
00:38:50,320 --> 00:38:53,340
Dive, dive, dive all in real.

477
00:38:53,341 --> 00:38:57,040
The crew has to react immediately and instinctively.

478
00:38:57,041 --> 00:39:00,160
If you've gone through realistic disaster drills,

479
00:39:00,161 --> 00:39:03,400
you're not likely to be scared and frozen in place

480
00:39:03,401 --> 00:39:06,580
during those critical first seconds when survival,

481
00:39:06,581 --> 00:39:08,300
maybe for the entire sub,

482
00:39:08,301 --> 00:39:10,740
is dependent on what each individual does.

483
00:39:10,741 --> 00:39:16,740
Today, submarining has become a much safer occupation

484
00:39:16,741 --> 00:39:20,860
because the disasters that have happened in the past,

485
00:39:20,861 --> 00:39:24,020
we have learned a very great deal about what can happen

486
00:39:24,021 --> 00:39:26,120
and how to prevent it from happening.

487
00:39:31,300 --> 00:39:34,540
In Halifax, Nova Scotia, the Canadian Navy

488
00:39:34,541 --> 00:39:37,260
trains its submariners with simulators

489
00:39:37,261 --> 00:39:40,260
to deal with the very kinds of trouble they experienced

490
00:39:40,261 --> 00:39:43,680
when a fire broke out in October of 2004

491
00:39:43,681 --> 00:39:47,460
on the HMCS Shikudami, killing a sailor.

492
00:39:49,560 --> 00:39:51,940
Simulators recreate the submarine environment

493
00:39:51,941 --> 00:39:53,180
in three dimensions.

494
00:40:02,080 --> 00:40:04,200
Equipped with flexible motion platforms

495
00:40:04,201 --> 00:40:06,860
and computer generated virtual systems,

496
00:40:06,861 --> 00:40:09,220
Simulators provide a realistic experience.

497
00:40:17,220 --> 00:40:20,880
Simulators are able to reproduce with amazing accuracy

498
00:40:20,881 --> 00:40:24,060
situations that are critical underwater

499
00:40:24,061 --> 00:40:26,320
that might endanger the life of the submarine and its crew.

500
00:40:27,160 --> 00:40:30,800
So the reality level at which the training is conducted

501
00:40:30,801 --> 00:40:32,720
is very, very high.

502
00:40:34,080 --> 00:40:36,040
You can feel what it's like to fail

503
00:40:36,041 --> 00:40:37,500
and know that you're dead,

504
00:40:38,100 --> 00:40:40,580
and then come back and feel what it's like to succeed

505
00:40:40,581 --> 00:40:42,560
and know that you've saved your own life

506
00:40:42,561 --> 00:40:43,560
and probably that of your crew.

507
00:40:44,180 --> 00:40:45,640
Full dive on the planes, two down.

508
00:40:49,560 --> 00:40:51,440
Simulator scenarios are all set up

509
00:40:51,441 --> 00:40:53,280
in the facilitator operating console.

510
00:40:57,720 --> 00:41:01,760
From here, we can control the majority of the faults

511
00:41:01,761 --> 00:41:04,020
and systems in the simulator.

512
00:41:04,021 --> 00:41:06,020
Pull ahead, pull down the plane.

513
00:41:06,021 --> 00:41:08,020
Pull out of the base, get on the plane.

514
00:41:08,021 --> 00:41:11,020
I'm actually going to trick the forward MG,

515
00:41:11,021 --> 00:41:14,020
a motor generator which provides half the electricity,

516
00:41:14,021 --> 00:41:17,020
AC electricity on the submarine.

517
00:41:17,021 --> 00:41:19,020
Two down.

518
00:41:22,020 --> 00:41:25,020
The loss of the motor generator will complicate

519
00:41:25,021 --> 00:41:27,020
an emergency go deep scenario,

520
00:41:27,021 --> 00:41:30,020
in which the men have to rapidly dive the sub

521
00:41:30,021 --> 00:41:32,020
in order to avoid collision with a surface vessel.

522
00:41:32,021 --> 00:41:46,021
In another training area, an engineer deals with the same emergency, but instead of controlling

523
00:41:47,520 --> 00:41:53,520
the sub, he's working with the engine.

524
00:41:53,521 --> 00:42:07,521
The emergency is over.

525
00:42:10,200 --> 00:42:13,900
The men have reacted to the situation with skill and confidence.

526
00:42:15,740 --> 00:42:18,540
In another part of the world, on a real submarine,

527
00:42:19,340 --> 00:42:23,080
sailors stay keenly focused on their tasks and take nothing for granted.

528
00:42:23,520 --> 00:42:24,220
One, three, five.

529
00:42:28,000 --> 00:42:31,980
Clearly, with training and rescue technology developed to such a high degree,

530
00:42:32,760 --> 00:42:34,580
submarines are safer than ever before.

531
00:42:35,560 --> 00:42:38,400
And while there will always be risks and unforeseen incidents,

532
00:42:39,060 --> 00:42:43,600
there will be men who are willing, even eager, to sign on for this special assignment.

533
00:42:44,680 --> 00:42:47,020
The submariner is considered the elite.

534
00:42:47,021 --> 00:42:53,200
So I think it's the romance of a submarine is what really attracts men to serve.

535
00:42:53,520 --> 00:42:58,020
submarine.

536
00:42:58,021 --> 00:43:00,960
And just as the sea seems limitless,

537
00:43:00,961 --> 00:43:04,360
so do the possibilities for submariners.

538
00:43:04,361 --> 00:43:08,560
The ocean, under the ocean, the sea bottom,

539
00:43:08,561 --> 00:43:13,500
is the last great unexplored territory on Earth.

540
00:43:13,501 --> 00:43:15,980
The technology that's been developed

541
00:43:15,981 --> 00:43:19,020
to put those submarines to sea, to keep them there,

542
00:43:19,021 --> 00:43:22,440
and to make them safe, is going to be technology

543
00:43:22,441 --> 00:43:29,440
that will be applied to the future exploration of that vast sea bottom.

544
00:43:31,220 --> 00:43:36,120
Every disaster has triggered new technology, new answers, and a new commitment

545
00:43:36,420 --> 00:43:40,880
to make sure that if a man survives in a submarine disaster for even a little while,

546
00:43:40,881 --> 00:43:42,040
that he can be rescued.

547
00:43:42,041 --> 00:43:52,000
As for those men who lost their lives,

548
00:43:52,640 --> 00:43:54,480
those twisted wrecks in which they lie

549
00:43:54,481 --> 00:43:57,360
are a poignant tribute to the daring and courage

550
00:43:57,361 --> 00:44:01,140
of what we've come to know as the silent service.

